# Force LF line endings on text fixtures whose SHA-256 is pinned in
# tests. Without this, Windows checkouts (default `core.autocrlf =
# true`) convert LF→CRLF on text files, breaking the byte-level hash
# `tests/vectors.rs:vector_file_sha256_matches_pin` asserts.
# NOTE: the corpus moved from tests/vectors/ to src/test_vectors/ — the
# rule below MUST track the file's live path or Windows CI hash-drifts.
crates/mk-codec/src/test_vectors/*.json text eol=lf

# Default everything else to native eol so Windows users still get
# their preferred line endings on Rust source.
* text=auto

# Committed `cargo vendor` tree (reproducible-builds-musl cycle, P3b) — store
# EVERY vendored file VERBATIM, byte-for-byte. `cargo vendor` writes a
# `.cargo-checksum.json` per crate carrying the SHA-256 of each file's ORIGINAL
# bytes; the `--locked --offline` reproducible build verifies those checksums.
# The `* text=auto` rule above would CRLF→LF-normalize the ~13 vendored files
# that ship with CRLF (e.g. `vendor/generic-array/*`, `vendor/zerocopy/win-cargo.bat`)
# on commit, mutating their bytes out from under the committed checksum → a
# clean checkout would fail the offline build with a checksum mismatch. `-text`
# disables all EOL normalization + binary/text detection for the vendor tree so
# the committed bytes match what `cargo vendor` emitted (and what the checksums
# pin). This rule MUST sort AFTER `* text=auto` (last-match-wins per attribute).
vendor/** -text
